                <pb facs="0846" n="750" sameAs="frus:frus1899/pg750"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Leishman to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Berne,
                                Switzerland</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-02-16"><hi rendition="#italic">February 16,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 131.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your dispatch No. 138, of
                    December 29, 1898, not having received any reply to my note of January 11
                    addressed to the President of the Swiss Confederation, copy of which was
                    inclosed to you in my dispatch No. 124, of January 14, 1899, I again addressed a
                        <pb facs="0847" n="751" xml:id="pg751"/> note to the President, under date
                    of February 14, 1899, calling attention to this fact and requesting a prompt
                    decision, in response to which I am just in receipt of a note from the Swiss
                    federal council containing a counter proposition and proposed projet of
                    convention, copies of which are herewith transmitted, together with translation,
                    which I hasten to forward by first mail, with the hope that it may reach you in
                    tune to enable you to submit the matter during the present session of the
                    Senate, in the event of the proposed changes in projet of convention meeting
                    with your approval; and as the note of the Swiss federal council is
                    self-explanatory it is hardly necessary for me to add any comments, and can only
                    regret that the delay in receiving a reply, which, considering the importance of
                    the matter, has not been unreasonable, leaves so little time for consideration
                    ere the closing of the present session of the Senate.</p>
                <p>Regretting my inability to more clearly carry out your wishes, I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John G. A.
                    Leishman.</hi></signed></closer>




